# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# __MKTRUE_NAME__ — copy to `.env` and fill in. Placeholders only in this file.
#
# Every var here is parsed by a typed config: `services/api/src/app/config.ts`
# for the API, `apps/web/lib/config.ts` for the page. A blank keyed var selects
# a **null adapter that refuses** (503) — never a permissive stub. In production
# the API refuses to boot without the four marked REQUIRED.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── The API's process ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The page's own port is fixed in `apps/web/package.json` (4200).
PORT=4201
NODE_ENV=development

# ── How the page reaches the API ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server-side only, and that is the whole topology: the browser never
# calls the API. It posts to this app's own route handlers under
# /api/access-request/*, which forward over the private network. So the API
# needs no CORS and no public router.
#
# **There is deliberately no NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL.** If you find yourself adding
# one, the browser is about to start talking to the API directly — and the
# limiter's trust in `x-client-ip` stops being sound in the same change.
#
# **Blank refuses, and no default exists in code:** the routes answer 503
# before reading the body, and /request-access is a 404. The value below is for
# this machine. Production names the API's private address, or leaves it blank
# to keep the request flow off.
API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4201

# ── Where this site itself lives ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The absolute origin. Every canonical, hreflang and share-card URL is built
# from it, and Open Graph has no notion of a relative URL: a crawler reading
# `/es/privacy` has nowhere to resolve it from. Defaulted in code so a clone
# builds, but set it in production or every one of those URLs points at the
# default host.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://__MKTRUE_NAME__.example.com

# ── Where the app itself lives ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# "Go to the app" links here. Public by definition — it ships in the client
# bundle, so it has to be set when the page is built, not only when it runs.
# Blank **hides** the CTA everywhere rather than rendering `href=""`, which
# still looks clickable (`hasApp`).
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://__MKTRUE_NAME__.example.com

# ── Who is behind the page ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Named on /about, /privacy and /terms, and in the footer's copyright line. The
# privacy page identifies a controller under GDPR, so leaving these blank is a
# legal shortcoming as much as a cosmetic one — the fallbacks exist so a clone
# builds, not so production can ship without them.
NEXT_PUBLIC_OWNER_NAME=
# Where a data-protection request goes. A **URL, not an address**: there is no
# __MKTRUE_NAME__.example.com mailbox, and a privacy page printing one that bounces is
# worse than one printing nothing. Blank falls back to "reply to the email you
# got from us".
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTACT_URL=

# ── The mailbox (Mongo) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Stores verified access requests. Verification itself is stateless, so
# this is only ever written *after* a request is verified — and persistence is
# best-effort, so a missing database degrades to "the mail still arrives"
# rather than failing the boot. Not required in production for that reason, and
# blank here: a fresh clone runs without a database.
#   mongodb://<user>:<password>@<host>:27017/__MKTRUE_NAME__?replicaSet=rs0&authSource=admin
# `authSource=admin` is REQUIRED when the user lives in admin, not in the
# database it reads: omitting it fails with an error that reads like a wrong
# password.
MONGO_URI=
MONGO_DB_NAME=__MKTRUE_NAME__

# ── Outbound mail (Resend) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Blank => the console mailer in development (prints the
# code so the flow is walkable), and a refusing null adapter in production —
# never a hardcoded fallback, and never the console mailer in production, where
# a "sent" that went to stdout is a request lost behind a success message.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION.
MAIL_FROM="no-reply@__MKTRUE_NAME__.example.com"
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Where a verified request is delivered — __MKTRUE_OWNER__'s
# address. Env-sourced and never client-influenced: a caller who could choose
# this would have an open relay.
ACCESS_REQUEST_DESTINATION_EMAIL=

# ── The daily mail ceiling ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Instance-wide, per UTC day, across every caller. The per-IP rate limit caps
# one address at 5 starts an hour and says nothing about the total; across
# enough addresses the daily volume is unbounded.
#
# **It counts mails, not requests**. A start reserves two: the code mail
# it sends now, and the delivery it commits to sending if the requester comes
# back with the code. `verify` reserves nothing, so nobody who has just proved
# they own an address is turned away for a mail already paid for. So 40 means at
# most 20 starts and 40 mails from ONE DAY'S ISSUANCE — but a token issued at
# 23:59 is verified after midnight, so a calendar day can carry up to half the
# limit again in deliveries deferred across it: the worst calendar day sends 60.
#
# Minimum usable value is 2, the cost of a single request. The API refuses to
# boot below that, and on anything that is not a whole number, because a ceiling
# it cannot parse silently disables the replay marker below.
#
# It also sizes that marker: a mailbox row remembers N+1 token digests.
# N+1 rather than half of it, because a token lives 15 minutes while this
# budget's key resets at midnight UTC — so a caller can hold one day's issuance
# and the next day's at the same time. Two days together issue at most N tokens,
# so N+1 cannot be cycled past. Lower this and the list shortens with it, on
# purpose — and note what that costs on the way down: the shorter cap trims
# digests off existing rows at the next claim, so any token still live at that
# moment can claim again, one delivery each, for up to one token lifetime. Verify
# consults no budget, so those deliveries escape the ceiling entirely.
#
# It matters because Resend's quota is per ACCOUNT, not per domain: without a
# ceiling, this page can silently exhaust an allowance something else
# sending from the same account depends on. 40 against a free tier of 100/day
# leaves deliberate headroom for whatever else sends from that account.
#
# Durable when MONGO_URI is set; per-process and reset-on-restart without it.
DAILY_SEND_LIMIT=40

# ── The stateless verification token ─────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. HMAC secret signing the pending-request token. The
# token IS the state — there is no Redis and no pending-requests collection.
# Rotate freely: it only invalidates codes in flight (15-minute TTL).
# Generate: `openssl rand -hex 32`
ACCESS_REQUEST_TOKEN_SECRET=

# ── What /health reports ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The running build's identity. The deploy supplies the checked-out commit.
GIT_SHA=
API_VERSION=0.1.0
